{
  "id": "1456e326-d6d8-4333-a50f-0ec2f8e31604",
  "productId": "openmrs",
  "title": "Visit Deletion Request Flow",
  "updatedAt": 1793548799035,
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "m958PWL4a0sCozRg",
      "productId": "openmrs",
      "question": "When a user deletes a visit in the patient chart, what code runs from the frontend request down to the database write? List the frontend function that sends the request, the server-side REST handler, the core service implementation, and the data-access method.",
      "generation": "g-b59a21",
      "kind": "grounded",
      "paragraphs": [
        {
          "text": "Deleting a visit is a **soft delete**. Nothing is ever removed from the `visit` table: React sends an HTTP `DELETE`, the OpenMRS REST module marks the row as *voided* (retired), and Hibernate writes the updated row back. Below is the exact chain, from the user's click to the database write.",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## The path in four steps",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "| Step | Where | What runs |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Frontend request | `openmrs-esm-patient-chart` | `deleteVisit()` in `visit.resource.tsx` [1] |\n| 2. REST handler | `openmrs-module-webservices.rest` | `VisitResource1_9.delete()` [2] |\n| 3. Core service | `openmrs-core` | `VisitServiceImpl.voidVisit()` [3] |\n| 4. Data access | `openmrs-core` | `HibernateVisitDAO.saveVisit()` [4] |",
          "citations": [
            1,
            2,
            3,
            4
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Step 1 — the user clicks \"Delete visit\" (patient chart)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The user opens the delete dialog from the visit action item, and the **Delete visit** button calls `initiateDeletingVisit` [5]. That function is provided by the `useDeleteVisit` hook [6], specifically at [7].",
          "citations": [
            5,
            6,
            7
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The function that actually sends the request is `deleteVisit(visitUuid)` in the visit resource module [1]. It calls the framework's `openmrsFetch` with `method: 'DELETE'` against `${restBaseUrl}/visit/${visitUuid}`, i.e. `DELETE /ws/rest/v1/visit/<uuid>`. Note this file is `visit.resource.tsx`, and the hook imports from `../visits-widget/visit.resource` [8].",
          "citations": [
            1,
            8
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "When the promise resolves, the hook clears the visit context if the deleted visit was the active one, invalidates the cached visit/encounter data via SWR, and shows a success snackbar with an **Undo** action [9]. Undo calls `restoreVisit`, which sends `POST /ws/rest/v1/visit/<uuid>` with body `{ voided: false }` [10].",
          "citations": [
            9,
            10
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Step 2 — the REST layer (webservices.rest module)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The request is routed (by convention over the resource name) to `VisitResource1_9`, annotated `@Resource(name = \"/visit\", supportedClass = Visit.class, ...)` [11]. Its `delete(Visit, String reason, RequestContext)` method [2] first checks whether the visit is already voided (in which case it just returns success), and otherwise delegates to the core service with `Context.getVisitService().voidVisit(visit, reason)` [12].",
          "citations": [
            11,
            2,
            12
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The visit itself is loaded earlier by `getByUniqueId(uuid)`, which calls `getVisitByUuid` on the service [13]. A separate `purge` method exists for hard deletes but is not what the standard `DELETE` runs [14].",
          "citations": [
            13,
            14
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Step 3 — the core service (openmrs-core)",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`VisitServiceImpl.voidVisit()` [3] is declared in the `VisitService` interface [15]. Its body is a single line: `return dao.saveVisit(visit);` [16]. The voiding itself (setting `voided`, `voidedBy`, `dateVoided`, `voidReason`) happens through the framework's `@Authorized` aspect / the `Visit` entity before this call; this method's job is simply to persist the already-modified object. Contrast this with `purgeVisit()` [17], which refuses to run if the visit still has encounters and then calls `dao.deleteVisit(visit)` — a real row removal.",
          "citations": [
            3,
            15,
            16,
            17
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Step 4 — the database write",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "`HibernateVisitDAO.saveVisit()` is the data-access method [4]. It is annotated `@Transactional` and returns `HibernateUtil.saveOrUpdate(getCurrentSession(), visit)` [18], which issues the SQL `UPDATE` on the `visit` row at transaction commit. The DAO method is declared in the `VisitDAO` interface [19].",
          "citations": [
            4,
            18,
            19
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "## Flow diagram",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "```mermaid\n---\ntitle: Delete Visit - Frontend to Database\n---\nsequenceDiagram\n    participant User\n    participant Dialog as DeleteVisitDialog\n    participant Hook as useDeleteVisit\n    participant Res as deleteVisit (visit.resource.tsx)\n    participant REST as VisitResource1_9.delete\n    participant Svc as VisitServiceImpl.voidVisit\n    participant DAO as HibernateVisitDAO.saveVisit\n    participant DB as visit table\n\n    User->>Dialog: Click \"Delete visit\"\n    Dialog->>Hook: initiateDeletingVisit()\n    Hook->>Res: deleteVisit(visitUuid)\n    Res->>REST: DELETE /ws/rest/v1/visit/<uuid>\n    REST->>REST: getByUniqueId + isVoided check\n    REST->>Svc: Context.getVisitService().voidVisit(visit, reason)\n    Svc->>DAO: dao.saveVisit(visit)\n    DAO->>DB: HibernateUtil.saveOrUpdate (UPDATE, sets voided=true)\n    DAO-->>REST: persisted Visit\n    REST-->>Hook: 204 No Content\n    Hook-->>User: success snackbar with Undo\n```",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "## One caveat",
          "citations": []
        },
        {
          "text": "The \"delete\" here is not a physical delete — the record stays in the database with its `voided` flag set, which is why the frontend can offer an Undo that simply flips the flag back. I traced this through the four files above; I did not read the `@Authorized` aspect in `openmrs-core` that populates the voiding audit fields before `voidVisit` is reached, so that claim rests on the entity class and OpenMRS conventions rather than on a file I opened in this session. The REST module and core files I cite are read from their default branches.",
          "citations": []
        }
      ],
